<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Edom</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 552 BCE (approx.) · capital Bozrah</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Edom, a kingdom of the arid highlands south of the Dead Sea, rich in copper and the incense trade, rival of Judah.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Edomites lived in the mountains east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabah">Arabah</a> valley, mentioned in Egyptian texts from the 13th century BCE. In the 10th century BCE large-scale <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> smelting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faynan">Faynan</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khirbat-en-nahas">Khirbat en-Nahas</a>, supported an emerging elite, and a kingdom with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bozrah">Bozrah</a> formed by the 8th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Edomite kings worshipped the god Qos and drew revenue from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> carrying Arabian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gaza">Gaza</a>. The Bible records wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah</a>. Edom became a loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>, and kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaus-gabri">Qaus-gabri</a> sent tribute and workers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Edom survived the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> but lost its independence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>, who campaigned there c. 552 BCE. Edomites later settled in southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Judah</a>, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaea">Idumaea</a>, while Arab groups and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nabataean-kingdom">Nabataean Kingdom</a> took their old lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1000 BCE – 552 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:39.80%;width:8.92%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 552 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bozrah</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qaus-malaku, Qaus-gabri</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Edomite religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Edomite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Edom, Edomites</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-776&amp;focus=edom" aria-label="Edom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5602 1056 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Edom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5922 1312L5908 1277M5922 1312L6092 1134M5922 1312L6140 1251"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5908 1277h0M6092 1134h0M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5922 1312h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5922 1312h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bozrah. <a href="/atlas?year=-776&amp;focus=edom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

